---
import Hero from "../components/Hero.astro";
import { PromptBlock } from "../components/PromptBlock";
import { StepNav } from "../components/StepNav";
import BaseLayout from "../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
import { PROMPTS, REPO_URL, SITE } from "../lib/site";
---

<BaseLayout>
	<Hero slot="hero" />
	<StepNav slot="nav" client:load />

	<section class="mb-12 grid gap-4">
		<p class="lede">
			{SITE.description}
		</p>
		<p class="lede">
			One prompt per step. Your agent writes the config and opens the pull request; you click the
			buttons and watch the isolation happen.
		</p>
	</section>

	{/* ─── Prereqs ─── */}
	<section id="prereqs" class="step">
		<header class="step-header">
			<h2 class="step-title">Before you start</h2>
		</header>

		<div class="step-body">
			<p>You need:</p>
			<ul>
				<li><strong class="text-kumo-default">Node.js</strong>, <code>git</code>, and a terminal</li>
				<li>
					<strong class="text-kumo-default">A coding agent</strong> — whatever you already use.{" "}
					<a href="https://opencode.ai">OpenCode</a> works fine if you don't have one.
				</li>
				<li>
					A <strong class="text-kumo-default">GitHub account</strong> — the deploy button creates a
					repo in it.
				</li>
				<li>
					A <strong class="text-kumo-default">free Cloudflare account</strong> — no credit card
					required.{" "}
					<a href="https://dash.cloudflare.com/sign-up">Sign up</a> now if you don't have one; it's
					one less thing to do mid-step.
				</li>
				<li>
					<strong class="text-kumo-default">Optional but helpful:</strong> the{" "}
					<a href="https://cli.github.com/">GitHub CLI</a> (<code>gh</code>), authenticated — lets
					your agent open the pull request for you in Step 3.
				</li>
			</ul>
		</div>
	</section>

	{/* ─── Step 1 ─── */}
	<section id="deploy" class="step">
		<header class="step-header">
			<h2 class="step-title"><span class="step-num-inline">1.</span>Deploy to Cloudflare</h2>
			<p class="step-lede">
				One click. A repo in your account, a database, and a production Worker — wired up for you.
			</p>
		</header>

		<div class="step-body mb-6">
			<p>Clicking the button below will:</p>
			<ul>
				<li>Create a copy of the app's repo in your own GitHub account</li>
				<li>Provision a production D1 database</li>
				<li>
					Connect <strong class="text-kumo-default">Workers Builds</strong> (Cloudflare's own
					CI/CD) to that repo
				</li>
				<li>Deploy the Worker</li>
			</ul>
			<p>No custom GitHub Actions to set up, no secrets to paste in — that part is automatic.</p>
		</div>

		<div class="mb-6 deploy-button-wrap">
			<a
				href={`https://deploy.workers.cloudflare.com/?url=${REPO_URL}`}
				target="_blank"
				rel="noopener noreferrer"
				class="deploy-button-link"
			>
				<img src="https://deploy.workers.cloudflare.com/button" alt="Deploy to Cloudflare" />
			</a>
		</div>

		<p class="checkpoint-line">
			<span class="checkpoint-line-mark" aria-hidden="true">✓</span>
			<span>
				<strong>You should see:</strong> open the production URL Cloudflare gives you — an
				Activity Log with three seeded entries. Remember what's in there; you'll compare it against
				your Preview shortly.
			</span>
		</p>
	</section>

	{/* ─── Step 2 ─── */}
	<section id="isolate" class="step">
		<header class="step-header">
			<h2 class="step-title"><span class="step-num-inline">2.</span>Isolate a resource</h2>
			<p class="step-lede">Give your branch its own database, so its Preview has real data to show.</p>
		</header>

		<div class="step-body mb-4">
			<p>First, get the repo onto your machine and make a branch — quick setup, nothing interesting yet:</p>
		</div>

		<div class="command-block">
			<div class="command-block-bar">
				<span class="command-block-label">Setup</span>
			</div>
			<pre><code>git clone https://github.com/&lt;you&gt;/d1-template-preview.git
cd d1-template-preview
git checkout -b isolate-preview-db</code></pre>
		</div>

		<div class="step-body mt-6 mb-6">
			<p>
				Now the actual point of this step. Previews inherit your <strong class="text-kumo-default"
					>code</strong
				> automatically — same branch, same routes. They do not inherit your{" "}
				<strong class="text-kumo-default">configuration</strong> — variables, secrets, and bindings
				like D1 come from a separate Previews configuration that starts <strong>empty</strong> unless
				someone sets one. This template hasn't, so your branch's Preview has no database bound yet
				at all.
			</p>
			<p>
				D1, KV, R2, and most other resources all work the same way: each is bound by an ID or name,
				and any two deployments pointing at the same ID share the same data — whether that's
				production and a Preview, or two different Previews. To isolate one, you bind it to a{" "}
				<em>different</em> resource. That's what you're about to do.
			</p>
		</div>

		<div class="step-body mb-6">
			<pre><code>{`{
  "d1_databases": [
    { "binding": "DB", "database_name": "d1-template-database" } // production
  ],
  "previews": {
    "d1_databases": [
      { "binding": "DB", "database_name": "workshop-preview-db" } // your branch's Preview
    ]
  }
}`}</code></pre>
		</div>

		<div class="step-body mb-6">
			<p>
				Same binding name (<code>DB</code>), different database — that's the whole mechanism. You
				could point future branches at this same database if you wanted them to share data, or give
				each its own; this workshop gives yours its own. Ask your agent to create the database and
				add that block:
			</p>
		</div>

		<PromptBlock label="Step 2 · Isolate a resource" text={PROMPTS.isolateResource} />

		<p class="checkpoint-line mt-5">
			<span class="checkpoint-line-mark" aria-hidden="true">✓</span>
			<span>
				<strong>You should see:</strong> <code>wrangler.json</code> now has a{" "}
				<code>previews.d1_databases</code> block pointing at a database that isn't the production
				one, and the change is committed.
			</span>
		</p>

		<details class="fallback mt-5">
			<summary>Why doesn't this apply to Durable Objects?</summary>
			<div class="fallback-body">
				<p>
					Durable Objects and Containers are the actual exception here. When the class lives in
					the same Worker, each Preview gets its own namespace and storage automatically — no
					override needed — because that state belongs to the Worker itself, not to an external
					resource identified by an ID. D1, KV, and R2 are the norm, not the exception: bind by ID,
					and anything sharing that ID shares the data.
				</p>
			</div>
		</details>
	</section>

	{/* ─── Step 3 ─── */}
	<section id="preview" class="step">
		<header class="step-header">
			<h2 class="step-title"><span class="step-num-inline">3.</span>Open your Preview</h2>
			<p class="step-lede">Push the branch, open a pull request, and Workers Builds does the rest.</p>
		</header>

		<div class="step-body mb-6">
			<p>Ask your agent to push and open the PR:</p>
		</div>

		<PromptBlock label="Step 3 · Open a pull request" text={PROMPTS.openPullRequest} />

		<p class="checkpoint-line mt-5">
			<span class="checkpoint-line-mark" aria-hidden="true">✓</span>
			<span>
				<strong>You should see:</strong> open the Preview URL — an <strong>empty</strong> Activity
				Log, no seeded rows. That's your new database, freshly created.
			</span>
		</p>

		<p class="checkpoint-line mt-4">
			<span class="checkpoint-line-mark" aria-hidden="true">→</span>
			<span>
				Keep this Preview tab open, along with your production tab from Step 1 — you'll flip
				between the two in the next step.
			</span>
		</p>
	</section>

	{/* ─── Step 4 ─── */}
	<section id="interact" class="step">
		<header class="step-header">
			<h2 class="step-title"><span class="step-num-inline">4.</span>Prove isolation</h2>
			<p class="step-lede">Don't take the empty list on faith. Write to it, then check production.</p>
		</header>

		<div class="step-body mb-6">
			<ol>
				<li>
					On your <strong>Preview</strong> tab, type something into <strong>Log an activity…</strong>{" "}
					and click <strong>Add entry</strong>.
				</li>
				<li>Switch to your <strong>production</strong> tab from Step 1 and refresh it.</li>
			</ol>
		</div>

		<p class="checkpoint-line">
			<span class="checkpoint-line-mark" aria-hidden="true">✓</span>
			<span>
				<strong>You should see:</strong> production still shows only its original three rows — the
				entry you just added is nowhere to be found there. Two deployments of the same code, two
				separate databases.
			</span>
		</p>

		<details class="fallback mt-5">
			<summary>Why does this matter?</summary>
			<div class="fallback-body">
				<p>
					This is what "isolated environment for every change" actually means in practice — not a
					slide, a database you can watch stay separate while you poke at it.
				</p>
			</div>
		</details>
	</section>

	{/* ─── Step 5 ─── */}
	<section id="observability" class="step">
		<header class="step-header">
			<h2 class="step-title"><span class="step-num-inline">5.</span>Observability</h2>
			<p class="step-lede">Your Preview has its own logs and traces too — not just its own database.</p>
		</header>

		<div class="step-body mb-6">
			<p>In the Cloudflare dashboard:</p>
			<ol>
				<li>Go to <strong>Workers &amp; Pages</strong> → your Worker.</li>
				<li>
					Open the <strong>Observability</strong> tab while on the Worker's{" "}
					<strong>production</strong> view, then switch to the <strong>Previews</strong> tab and
					select your branch.
				</li>
				<li>Refresh your Preview URL a couple of times, then check the request list.</li>
			</ol>
		</div>

		<p class="checkpoint-line">
			<span class="checkpoint-line-mark" aria-hidden="true">✓</span>
			<span>
				<strong>You should see:</strong> requests and logs scoped to your branch's Preview —
				separate from production's traffic, same as the database.
			</span>
		</p>
	</section>

	{/* ─── Step 6 ─── */}
	<section id="final" class="step">
		<header class="step-header">
			<h2 class="step-title"><span class="step-num-inline">6.</span>TBD</h2>
			<p class="step-lede">Pending a conversation with Yomna.</p>
		</header>

		<div class="callout callout--warn">
			<span class="callout-icon" aria-hidden="true">?</span>
			<div>
				<p class="callout-title">Not decided yet</p>
				<p class="callout-body">
					This is a genuine placeholder, not a mistake — the final step of the workshop is still
					being worked out with Yomna (PM). Don't build content into this slot until that's
					settled.
				</p>
			</div>
		</div>
	</section>

	{/* ─── Wrap-up ─── */}
	<section id="wrap-up" class="step">
		<header class="step-header">
			<h2 class="step-title">Clean up</h2>
		</header>

		<div class="step-body">
			<p>
				Closing or merging your pull request deletes the Preview deployment automatically. It does{" "}
				<strong class="text-kumo-default">not</strong> delete the D1 database you created in Step 2
				— that's a standalone account resource that outlives the branch. Delete it yourself once
				you're done:
			</p>
		</div>

		<div class="command-block mt-5">
			<div class="command-block-bar">
				<span class="command-block-label">Cleanup</span>
			</div>
			<pre><code>npx wrangler d1 delete workshop-preview-db</code></pre>
		</div>
	</section>
</BaseLayout>
